7525947,2023-09-10,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"To Whom It May Concern, I trust this letter finds you well. I am writing to discuss important matters related to my rights and privacy under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, specifically 15 USC 1681.

Section 602 ( a ) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act emphasizes the need to ensure that consumer reporting agencies, such as XXXX, Equifax and XXXX, carry out their responsibilities with fairness, impartiality, and respect for the consumers right to privacy. I wish to assert my rights as a consumer to safeguard my private information, as reinforced by 15 USC 6801.

Under 15 USC 6801, it is the policy of Congress that financial institutions, including furnishers of information to credit agencies, have an affirmative and continuous obligation to respect customer privacy and protect the security and confidentiality of nonpublic personal information.

However, I would like to draw attention to Section 604 ( a ) ( 2 ) of 15 USC 1681, which stipulates that a consumer reporting agency may furnish a consumer report only in accordance with the written instructions of the respective consumer. I have not provided written consent for XXXX, Equifax or XXXX, or the furnisher of information to credit agencies, to share my information and I hereby revoke any previous consent, be it verbal, non-verbal, written, implied or otherwise.

Moreover, 15 USC 6802 ( b ) ( c ) mandates that a financial institution must inform the consumer of their right to exercise nondisclosure options. I was never informed of this right by the furnisher of information to credit agencies.

Additionally, 15 USC 1681 ( a ) ( 5 ) prohibits consumer reporting agencies from including certain adverse information in consumer reports without authorization. This account constitutes adverse information being reported without my permission, which is in violation of the law.

Furthermore, 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2 ( A ) ( 1 ) ( A ) states that information furnished to consumer reporting agencies must be accurate. I have reason to believe that the information being reported is inaccurate.

Lastly, 15 U.S. Code 1681e mandates that consumer reporting agencies maintain reasonable procedures to avoid violations and limit the furnishing of consumer reports for specific purposes. XXXX, Equifax and XXXX do not appear to be maintaining such reasonable procedures. 


Additionally, 12 CFR 1016.7 affirms that a consumer may exercise their right to opt out at any time. I am hereby exercising my right to opt out of your reporting services.

I kindly request that you address these concerns promptly and take the necessary actions to rectify the violations of my rights as outlined in the Fair Credit Reporting Act.",,"EQUIFAX, INC.",NV,89107,,Web,2023-09-10,Closed with explanation,Yes
